 ## Background
-The method was proposed by researchers from Bell Labs and the State University of New York at Stony Brook in 1998 [S1, unofficial]. It builds on the concept of using wavelet transforms for volume visualization, previously explored by Muraki and Gross et al. [S1, unofficial]. The algorithm addresses the limitations of Westermann's method, which required traversing wavelet coefficients and on-the-fly reconstruction, and traditional pyramid/octree structures which involved expensive traversal [S1, unofficial]. It leverages the time-frequency locality of wavelets, which provide variable window widths (narrow for high frequencies, wide for low frequencies), making them superior to Short-Time Fourier Transform (STFT) for detecting local frequency changes [S1, unofficial].
+The method was proposed by researchers from Bell Labs and implemented within the VolVis system at SUNY Stony Brook in 1998 [S1, unofficial]. It builds on the concept of using wavelet transforms for volume visualization, previously explored by Muraki and Gross et al. [S1, unofficial]. The algorithm addresses the limitations of Westermann's method, which required traversing wavelet coefficients and on-the-fly reconstruction, and traditional pyramid/octree structures which involved expensive traversal [S1, unofficial]. It leverages the time-frequency locality of wavelets, which provide variable window widths (narrow for high frequencies, wide for low frequencies), making them superior to Short-Time Fourier Transform (STFT) for detecting local frequency changes [S1, unofficial].
 
